{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 25,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.072,
            0.159,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "110"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.111,
            0.478,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "Consider the linear equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.464,
            0.132,
            0.879,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "x - y = 1 0\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.154,
            0.879,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "10x - 10y = 100\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.182,
            0.882,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "To eliminate x, we multiply the first equation by 10. But by doing this, both equations become $10x - 10y = 100$ . Since both equations are the same, every solution of $10x - 10y = 100$ is a solution of both equations. We know that $10x - 10y = 100$ has infinitely many solutions. Hence, the given pair of equations has infinitely many solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.298,
            0.4,
            0.322
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.335,
            0.598,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "How many solutions are there to the equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.358,
            0.636,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "10x-5y=20  and  4x-2y=8?",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.381,
            0.854,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": "Give 3 more examples of pairs of equations that lead to $0 = 0$ after subtraction, and therefore have infinitely many solutions. Can you find a simple rule to check when this happens?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.453,
            0.55,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "Consider a general pair of equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.477,
            0.589,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "a_{1}x + b_{1}y + c_{1} = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.503,
            0.59,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "a_{2}x + b_{2}y + c_{2} = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.528,
            0.551,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "with $a _ { _ 2 } , b _ { _ 2 }$ and $c _ { 2 }$ each not equal to 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.553,
            0.883,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "From the discussion above, it can be seen that whenever one of the equations is a constant multiple of another, the equations are the same and so have an infinite number of solutions. Suppose this constant multiple is k, where k is not necessarily an integer but may be any number. Then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.304,
            0.654,
            0.703,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "a_{1}=ka_{2},b_{1}=kb_{2},c_{1}=kc_{2},where k\\neq 0.",
          "image_source": "images/page_25_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.682,
            0.881,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "Thus, when $\\frac{a_{1}}{a_{2}}=\\frac{b_{1}}{b_{2}}=\\frac{c_{1}}{c_{2}}=k,$ the pair of equations has infinitely many solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.746,
            0.399,
            0.77
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.784,
            0.437,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "What if one of $a _ { _ 2 } , b _ { _ 2 } , c _ { _ 2 }$ is zero?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.827,
            0.399,
            0.851
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.864,
            0.851,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "Does a pair of linear equations always have either a unique solution or infinitely many solutions?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
